---
/**
 * plan.md §8.2：这个页面是「指南 + 自证」合体。
 * 写作口径必须是 `tested to 16 CFR 1633`，不是 `1633 compliant`。
 * 报告原件未到位前不要发布任何合规宣称。
 */
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { pageTitle } from '@/lib/seo';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '@/lib/schema';

const title = pageTitle('16 CFR 1633 Hotel Mattress Compliance');
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', path: '/guides/' },
  { name: '16 CFR 1633 hotel mattress', path: '/guides/16-cfr-1633-hotel-mattress/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description="What 16 CFR 1633 requires of a hotel mattress, how prototype testing works, and which documents a buyer should ask for before signing."
  jsonld={[breadcrumbSchema(crumbs)]}
>
  <div class="container-page py-8">
    <Breadcrumbs
      items={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' }, { name: '16 CFR 1633' }]}
    />
  </div>

  <article class="container-page max-w-3xl pb-16">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
      16 CFR 1633 hotel mattress compliance
    </h1>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
      <p>
        If you are buying mattresses for a US hotel, the spec sheet will contain the phrase
        16 CFR 1633, and the person who put it there will probably not explain it. Short version:
        it is the federal open-flame standard for mattress sets sold in the United States. It has
        been in force since 2007, and a mattress that has not been qualified against it cannot
        legally be sold there.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What the test measures</h2>
      <p>
        The test procedure is set out in 16 CFR §1633.7. California's TB 129 was the method it
        grew out of; the federal rule is a modified version, and the two are not interchangeable.
        A burner is applied to the side of a mattress set and the lab records how much heat the
        assembly releases over a 30-minute test. Two limits apply:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
        <li>Peak heat release rate no higher than 200 kW, at any point in the 30-minute test</li>
        <li>Total heat release no higher than 15 MJ in the first ten minutes</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Those are the numbers the fire barrier is designed around. A barrier that slows ignition
        but keeps releasing heat will fail on the second figure even if the first one looks fine.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Prototype testing, and why it matters to a buyer</h2>
      <p>
        Manufacturers do not test every mattress. They test a prototype, meaning one specific
        construction: a defined core, a defined foam, a defined barrier. Production units can
        then be sold without further testing if they differ from that prototype only in ways
        the rule allows.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the part buyers miss. A report on file does not automatically cover the mattress
        on your purchase order. It covers the build that was tested. If the supplier changed the
        foam density, the barrier weight, or the spring unit after the test, the report may no
        longer apply to what is being shipped.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the useful question is not "do you have a 1633 report". It is "does the mattress I am
        ordering fall inside the prototype that report covers". Ask for the prototype description
        and compare it to the specification. A supplier who cannot answer that has not thought
        about it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Widths and depths</h2>
      <p>
        The rule allows a mattress to be sold without retesting when it differs from the tested
        prototype in length and width, twin to king. Depth is not covered by that allowance, and
        a change of foam, barrier or spring unit is a separate question that has to be justified
        or tested. So the build described in the report matters more than the size printed on
        it. Ask which widths and depths the factory has qualified against the prototype, and
        get that in writing for anything outside it. The coil count changes with the width even
        when the build is nominally the same.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Who carries the obligation</h2>
      <p>
        In practice the US importer or the seller carries the liability, which is why they ask
        foreign suppliers for paperwork rather than a statement of intent. CPSC can stop goods at
        the border, and a non-compliant mattress found in a property is a problem for the party
        that sold it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each mattress also carries a permanent label stating compliance with Part 1633. That
        label is not decoration. If the document trail is weak, the label is the thing an
        inspector reads first.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">The other standard: 16 CFR 1632</h2>
      <p>
        1633 covers open flame. 16 CFR 1632 covers cigarette ignition, and it applies to
        mattresses and mattress pads rather than mattress sets. It is a separate test (TB 106) and
        a separate report.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your order includes mattress protectors or pads, ask about 1632 as well. A 1633 report
        does not cover them.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Three ways to word a claim</h2>
      <p>
        Suppliers describe fire performance in ways that sound similar and are not. It helps to
        know which one you are reading.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto pt-2">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[34rem] border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-left text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-600">
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">Wording</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">What it means</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 font-semibold">What it needs</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-ink-900">"Fire-retardant materials"</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-600">A description of what is inside</td>
              <td class="py-3 text-ink-600">Nothing. It is not a compliance claim</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-ink-900">"Tested to 16 CFR 1633"</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-600">A test happened on a defined prototype</td>
              <td class="py-3 text-ink-600">The report itself</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-ink-900">"16 CFR 1633 compliant"</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-600">A legal conclusion about this product</td>
              <td class="py-3 text-ink-600">Evidence that this SKU sits inside a tested prototype</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The middle one is the safest claim a supplier can make and the easiest to verify. The
        third is not dishonest, but it needs the SKU comparison behind it. A supplier who uses
        the third wording and cannot produce the second is worth a follow-up question.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Checklist for a buyer</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>The test report, with the laboratory's name and ID.</li>
        <li>The prototype description: core, foam, barrier, and the sizes covered.</li>
        <li>A comparison showing your ordered widths and depths inside that prototype.</li>
        <li>A 1632 report if the order includes pads or mattress protectors.</li>
        <li>Confirmation that the build has not changed since the test date.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="pt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What we can supply</h2>
      <p>
        Our mattress barrier is 220 g fire-retardant cotton and 90 g fire-retardant foam over the
        pocket-spring unit, and the build is tested to 16 CFR 1633 at SGS Shunde, report
        SDFTS25002706R05_EN. Ask for it with your quote. Note what the report does and does not
        say: one prototype, tested at 99 × 191 × 25 cm without a foundation, with no size range
        stated. For other sizes we ask the manufacturer to confirm coverage in writing before
        quoting, rather than send a PDF and leave you to work it out.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the project is outside the US, tell us which standard applies. BS 7177 and EN 597 are
        different tests and a US report says nothing about them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Test report available on request</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-600">
        Wholesale inquiries only. Send the widths, depths and destination and we will reply with
        the report and the prototype comparison.
      </p>
      <a href="/request-a-quote/" class="mt-4 inline-block rounded-md bg-brand-600 px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white">
        Request the test report
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
